#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124134 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "045854fc-9cad-5005-a306-9550848e25df" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Critical Points of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "593eeb42-56b8-5177-8a9f-38d08a5186c1" :question/math-academy-id "e-12584" :question/problem "Determine the critical points of $f(x,y) = x^3+y^3-3x^2y+9y.$" :question/worked-solution "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $\\mathbf a$ in the domain of $f$ such that $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) =\\mathbf 0^T$ or $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a)$ does not exist, where\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/db60be51a74c53caf3495a85e960be92ca8173c24c168e0ba30e335ef163be9e.png)\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =3{x}^{2}-6xy=3x(x-2y) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =3{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/2faec0235882f18aa523890be6f7f52f446219ba9a6c27fe8f8179ae90050d0d.png)\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ such that $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T$ or $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T.$\n\nSetting $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T,$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x(x-2y)=0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}+9=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain $x=0$ or $x=2y.$ Now, we substitute these values into the second equation.\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n3y^2 - 3(0)^2 + 9 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad y^2 = -3,\n$$\n\nbut this last equation has no real solution.\n\n- For $x = 2y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n3y^2 - 3(2y)^2 + 9 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad y = \\pm 1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into $x = 2y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=-1 & \\,⟹\\,x=2(-1)=-2 \\\\ y=1 & \\,⟹\\,x=2(1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(-2,-1)$ and $(2,1).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has two critical points, $(-2,-1)$ and $(2,1).$ Note that since $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf 0^T$ at these two points, they are also both stationary points."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124135 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca319c5-9f62-51e3-b670-611fc8a8daaa" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Critical Point Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"}] :question/id #uuid "7c9f4b9e-be60-5da1-a140-08a3f183ce0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12585" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y) \n%= 2x + 2y - x^2 + y^2 + 2$\nhas the first derivative\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/083732de2321f70e27269ba55cec2a744a9424d443f68b4948caf313d30ae630.png)\nand has a stationary point at $(1,-1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $H(1,-1) > 0$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(1,-1) <    0$\n\n- The point $(1,-1)$ is a saddle point of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $\\mathbf{a}\\in\\mathbb{R}^2$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(\\mathbf a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) > 0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) <                0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $\\mathbf{a}$\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) >0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $\\mathbf{a}$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) <       0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $\\mathbf{a}$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) = 0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/d680921af1b938e22a2705516c9f463cbb8b7bdb3ab1646b4613f6191e2a06f2.png) we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 2-2x, \n\\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} =2+2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2-2x)=-2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2+2y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2+2y)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,-1)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of $\\boldsymbol f''(1,-1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,-1) = \\det(\\boldsymbol f''(1,-1)) = -2 \\cdot 2 - 0^2 = -4 < 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(1,-1) = (\\boldsymbol f''(1,-1))_{11} = -2 \\lt 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(1,-1) < 0,$ the point $(1,-1)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124136 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61cadd46-5fae-51c6-8e5b-8114e48b12da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding and Classifying All Critical Points Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"}] :question/id #uuid "e5398577-d70c-56f9-9c89-4a9d4e8f1d45" :question/math-academy-id "e-12586" :question/problem "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/47070dbaf25d08b50c3f42c5f4ef9ce7d02b4e541112fa3049126f2d5cdcffed.png) \ngiven that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/7e7f250d8444de28d127551ad1024b9b829d8ce8851cdb644efc69ad9469ce6d.png)" :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/849819e826aeb3943d8aa6e489d270d71b85ed63f77c50f1b84ab0b93a399e1e.png) occurs at its critical points. A point ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/e604f9fa7e2d6f44f89ab4e5d557b403fdcd86b7203502d0d7a71d0f4085a43d.png) in the domain of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/483e14a283d6074afea743287da4d8a82af7d6fb44d37fad07e656580dd3d808.png) is a critical point of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/8538defb8f14b78578b1e76a85947d6f73821c3c6bd13c8e12c22925b2459574.png) if ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/0ea5054ca54fa11a9c5a0f9d9bb62d1687ab226d9c28ae4f165515cd65bcbccc.png) (for stationary points) or ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/f49532342fc531783f16cecbbf3ec4de8d8344339289530641fe944998ccefdf.png) doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/cbf525e2f109ea8dde911a201ee6a5b426360c104882c0933ae26d5118526